You are given an n x m map of a building, and your task is to count the number of its connected rooms formed by floor squares where you can walk left, right, up, and down.

Input:
The first input line has two integers n and m: the height and width of the map.
The next n lines have m characters describing the map: '.' (floor) or '#' (wall).

Output:
Print one integer: the number of rooms.

Constraints:
1<=n, m<=1000

Strategy:
This is classic intro bfs island finder. We run a loop through all the point and start a bfs wherever theres a '.'. We then spread in all directions and end when there's a '#' while changing the dots into '#' states so they're marked visited. Everytime we start the bfs we increase a counter, the count is the number of islands which is the number of rooms.